      "latency_ms": 2201.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning each water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use to irrigate crops.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the air as humidity, fog, or steam from a boiling kettle.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Magical (Chemically Speaking)\nWater has several unusual scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water dissolves more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (in our blood, in rivers, and through plant roots).\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of a giant tree against gravity).\n*   **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, they become denser and sink. Water, however, expands and becomes *less* dense when it freezes. This means ice floats on top of lakes and oceans, insulating the water below so marine life can survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology and water are inseparable. \n*   **Inside Us:** The human body is made of about 60% water. Our brains are roughly 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones contain around 31%. We need water to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, and digest food"
